import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.List;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;

// JPA Entity -- maps to the "products" database table
@Entity
@Table(name = "products")
public class Product {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private String name;

    @Column(name = "price", precision = 10, scale = 2)
    private double price;

    @Column(name = "category")
    private String category;

    // JPA requires a no-arg constructor
    protected Product() {}

    public Product(String name, double price, String category) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
        this.category = category;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public double getPrice() { return price; }
}

// Spring Data JPA -- no SQL, no boilerplate, implementation is generated
public interface ProductRepository extends JpaRepository<Product, Long> {
    // Spring derives SQL from the method name at runtime:
    // SELECT * FROM products WHERE price < ?
    List<Product> findByPriceLessThan(double maxPrice);

    // SELECT * FROM products WHERE category = ?
    List<Product> findByCategory(String category);
}

ORM addresses repeated work such as turning rows into objects, tracking changes, and representing associations. It also introduces rules about entity identity, fetching, cascading, and persistence-context scope.

A small stack looks like this:

- application/service code;
- Spring Data repository, if used;
- Jakarta Persistence API;
- provider such as Hibernate;
- JDBC driver;
- relational database.

JPQL and entity mappings improve portability, but schemas, SQL dialects, locking, generated keys, and performance behavior still vary. JPA is an abstraction, not a promise that database differences disappear.

```java
// Spring Boot 3.2 + Java 17 — note jakarta.persistence (not javax)
import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.*;

@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
    private String email;

    private String name;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private List<Order> orders = new ArrayList<>();
    // constructors, getters, setters omitted
}

@Repository
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    Optional<User> findByEmail(String email); // Spring derives the SQL
}

@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;
    UserService(UserRepository r) { this.userRepository = r; }

    public User createUser(String email, String name) {
        var user = new User();
        user.setEmail(email);
        user.setName(name);
        return userRepository.save(user); // INSERT generated for you
    }
}
```
The annotations are the entire mapping — no SQL string anywhere.

