JPA still issues SQL through JDBC — you just stopped writing it. For a 100k-row batch import, you'd drop back to JDBC batch inserts for speed.

```java
@Transactional
public void update(User detached) {
    detached.setName("New Name");
    entityManager.persist(detached); // EntityExistsException / detached error
    // persist is only for brand-new (transient) entities
}
```

```java
@Transactional
public User update(User detached) {
    detached.setName("New Name");
    User managed = entityManager.merge(detached); // SELECT + UPDATE
    return managed; // use the returned managed copy, not 'detached'
}
```
Spring Data's `save()` does this automatically: it calls `persist` for new entities and `merge` for existing ones.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager entityManager;

    @Transactional
    public Order createOrder(Long userId, String item) {
        var user = entityManager.find(User.class, userId); // SELECT by PK
        if (user == null) throw new UserNotFoundException();
        var order = new Order();
        order.setUser(user);
        order.setItem(item);
        entityManager.persist(order); // INSERT queued until commit
        return order;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void updateStatus(Long orderId, OrderStatus status) {
        var order = entityManager.find(Order.class, orderId);
        order.setStatus(status); // dirty checking → UPDATE, no save() call
    }

    @Transactional
    public void importAll(List<Order> orders) {
        for (int i = 0; i < orders.size(); i++) {
            entityManager.persist(orders.get(i));
            if (i % 50 == 0) {           // batch: avoid OOM
                entityManager.flush();
                entityManager.clear();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

